<article>
	<h1><span>Language Manager: Edit Override</span></h1>

	<ul class="nav">
		<li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
		<li><a href="#fields">Fields</a></li>
		<li><a href="#search">Search</a></li>
		<li><a href="#toolbar">Toolbar</a></li>
	</ul>

	<section id="overview">
		<h2><span>Overview</span></h2>

		<p>Here you create or edit a language override: text that replaces the text of one language constant. The
			form on the left holds the override. The panel on the right searches the known language strings so you
			can find the constant to override.</p>
	</section>
	<section id="fields">
		<h2><span>Fields</span></h2>

		<p>The fields are under <strong>Create a New Override</strong>, or <strong>Edit this Override</strong>
			when an existing override is open.</p>

		<dl>
			<dt>Language Constant (Required)</dt>
			<dd>The language constant of the string you want to override. Each language output text on your site is
				identified by a specific language constant which you have to use for creating an override of the
				text.</dd>

			<dt>Text</dt>
			<dd>The text which you want to be displayed instead of the overridden one. There may be placeholders
				(e.g. %s, %d or %1$s) in the text which are replaced by other texts before displaying, so you should
				leave them in there.</dd>

			<dt>For both locations</dt>
			<dd>A checkbox, shown only when the override's location is administrator. When ticked, the override
				is saved for both the site and the administrator.</dd>

			<dt>Language</dt>
			<dd>Read only. The language for which the constant is overridden: the language chosen in the filter on
				the Overrides screen.</dd>

			<dt>Location</dt>
			<dd>Read only. Whether the override is for the site (frontend) or administrator (backend), as chosen in
				the filter on the Overrides screen.</dd>

			<dt>File</dt>
			<dd>Read only. The INI file the override is stored in.</dd>
		</dl>
	</section>
	<section id="search">
		<h2><span>Search</span></h2>

		<p>The panel <strong>Search text you want to change</strong> searches the known language strings.</p>

		<dl>
			<dt>Search for</dt>
			<dd>Whether to search constant names (<strong>Constant</strong>) or the texts (<strong>Value</strong>).
				<strong>Value</strong> is selected at first.</dd>

			<dt>Search text box and Search button</dt>
			<dd>Enter the text to look for and click <strong>Search</strong>.</dd>

			<dt>Search Results</dt>
			<dd>Matching strings, ten at a time, each showing its constant and text. Click <strong>More
				Results</strong> to load the next ten. Click a result to copy its constant into
				<strong>Language Constant</strong> and its text into <strong>Text</strong>. <em>No matching texts
				found</em> is shown when nothing matches.</dd>
		</dl>
	</section>
	<section id="toolbar">
		<h2><span>Toolbar</span></h2>
		<p>At the top right you will see the toolbar.</p>
		<p>The functions are:</p>
		<dl>
			<dt>Save</dt>
			<dd>Saves the override and stays on this screen. Shown only to users who may edit.</dd>

			<dt>Save &amp; Close</dt>
			<dd>Saves the override and returns to the list of overrides. Shown only to users who may edit.</dd>

			<dt>Save &amp; New</dt>
			<dd>Saves the override and opens an empty form for a new one. Shown only to users who may both edit and
				create.</dd>

			<dt>Cancel / Close</dt>
			<dd>Returns to the list of overrides without saving. The button reads <strong>Cancel</strong> for a new
				override and <strong>Close</strong> for an existing one.</dd>

			<dt>Help</dt>
			<dd>Opens this help screen.</dd>
		</dl>
	</section>
</article>
